Deterministic Simulation Testing (DST) tests
=============================================

See docs/M_DST.md for the design.  DST runs the REAL multi-loop,
work-stealing executor and shared-everything, fiber-yielding-latch data
plane deterministically by serializing the N loops as N cooperatively-
scheduled entities on ONE OS thread under a seed-determined
interleaving: the identical concurrency code runs; only WHICH runnable
loop advances next, and when virtual time advances, are decided by the
seed -- so a run replays byte-for-byte from (seed, config).

Tests
-----

test_sim_rng.c
    Phase 0/1 unit test: the seeded PRNG tree (reproducibility,
    per-stream independence) and the virtual clock.  Runs in the
    ordinary `make check` -- sim is dormant in a normal build, so these
    properties are checked without a sim build.

test_sim_sched.c  (needs a sim build)
    Phase 3: a 4-loop, 16-worker executor with cross-loop spawn, work
    stealing, and fiber yields, driven by xtc_sim_exec_run.  Asserts the
    run reaches quiescence on one thread (no hang) and that the same
    seed replays an identical completion-order hash while a different
    seed reorders.

test_sim_pingpong.c  (needs a sim build)
    Phase 3: N ping/pong pairs where each ping is on a DIFFERENT loop
    from its pong, so each ping PARKS on xtc_recv awaiting a cross-loop
    reply.  Exercises the mailbox park/wake path across loops under the
    deterministic scheduler; asserts full completion + identical replay.

test_sim_fault.c  (needs a sim build)
    Phase 5: N workers each consult xtc_sim_fault at a decision point;
    asserts the faulted-worker set replays from the seed and that the
    state hash is stable whether faults are on or off (the FAULT PRNG
    stream is isolated from the scheduling streams).

test_sim_soak.c  (needs a sim build)
    Phase 6: a configurable seed sweep (argv[1], default 200) over a
    mixed workload -- many cross-loop ping/pong pairs (mailbox park/wake
    across loops) plus timer-driven sleepers (the virtual clock).  For
    each seed it runs twice and asserts the run reaches quiescence,
    replays identically (state hash + application result), holds the
    per-step invariants, and that the sweep explores many distinct
    schedules.  This test surfaced a real scheduler liveness bug (timer
    parks never woke -- see docs/M_DST.md).

test_sim_machine_death.c  (needs a sim build)
    Machine-death / proc-kill under DST.  A seeded reaper proc, after a
    seeded virtual-time delay, kills a victim with xtc_exit_pid (the
    BEAM-style async kill: the victim raises its exit at the next
    yield/recv, running the real __notify_links_and_monitors path).
    PART A verifies exit PROPAGATION: a linked+monitoring observer
    receives the 'E' exit signal and the 'D' monitor DOWN, the run
    quiesces, and it replays (signal count + order hash + sim state
    hash).  PART B verifies SUPERVISOR RESTART: a one_for_one supervisor
    deterministically restarts a killed permanent child (restart count
    replays), then the supervisor is stopped + joined so the run winds
    down cleanly (no leak under ASan).  A different seed kills a
    different victim/child and stays consistent.

test_sim_swarm.c  (needs a sim build)
    Swarm / soak fleet: a large, SHARDABLE seed sweep over the rich set
    of fault scenarios the sim models -- for each seed a mixed workload
    (cross-loop ping/pong + timer sleepers) with a seeded combination of
    network partition, seeded delivery latency, Buggify, and a
    machine-death kill.  Each seed runs twice and must reach quiescence,
    hold the per-step invariants, and replay (state hash + app result);
    the sweep must explore many distinct schedules.  Every run frees all
    per-run state (fresh exec + partition/buggify cleared) so a large
    sweep stays memory-bounded (RSS flat across seeds).

        test_sim_swarm                 -- bounded default (300 seeds),
                                          for make check / the CI job.
        test_sim_swarm <count>         -- sweep <count> seeds from 0.
        test_sim_swarm <count> <base>  -- shard: <count> seeds from
                                          <base> (disjoint per shard).

    A nightly/manual 100k+ sweep runs several disjoint shards, e.g.

        test_sim_swarm 100000 0      &
        test_sim_swarm 100000 100000 &
        test_sim_swarm 100000 200000 &   # ... etc.

    (~90 s per 100k shard; a few MB RSS each.)  The swarm surfaced a
    real use-after-free in the sim net-latency deferred-delivery path
    (a delayed cross-loop message to a proc killed inside the deferral
    window dereferenced the freed proc); fixed by re-resolving the
    target by pid at fire time -- see docs/M_DST.md.

Running the sim-build tests
---------------------------

The sched/pingpong tests require the deterministic-simulation I/O
backend, which is a separate build (the backend is single-per-binary):

    XTC_SRC_DIR=<repo> CC=gcc sh test/sim/run_sim_tests.sh

The harness builds libxtc with --with-io-backend=sim, then compiles and
runs both tests.  A CI job (sim-dst) runs this on every push.

What DST does and does not catch
--------------------------------

CAUGHT: ordering/logic races, lock-discipline races (lost wakeup,
double-grant, FIFO violation), message races, use-after-free on
scheduling boundaries (run under ASan), liveness/deadlock, timer
ordering.

NOT CAUGHT: pure hardware memory-ordering races (missing/weak
memory_order, store-buffer reorder) -- those remain the domain of
ThreadSanitizer + the CI sanitizer jobs.  Sim DST owns the logical
concurrency-correctness space; TSan owns the physical memory-ordering
space.  They are complementary.
